  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday through Thursday)\nIssued at 110 PM MDT Fri Oct 9 2026\n\nMonday through Tuesday continues to see deterministic model suites \ntrend toward the aforementioned dryslot remaining mostly dominant \nover NM, lodged between the larger scale synoptic trough to the W \nand NW and the remnants of what is now Hurricane Simon being \npulled northward over northern Mexico and through the TX Big Bend \nregion. There remains notable differences b/w deterministic model \nruns with the ECMWF being more progressive in tracking the H5 to \nthe N/NW to southwest WY by midnight Tuesday night. The latest 12Z\nGFS run has become more progressive as well, tracking the H5 low \nsteadily to the east over northern AZ and southeastern UT by the \nsame time frame. The prior 06Z GFS run had to low stalling over AZ\nand progressing over NM much later by Wednesday night. The \nremaining question is how much of Hurricane Simon's remnant \nmoisture can still reach southeastern NM Tuesday night. So there \nremains relatively low forecast confidence regarding rain chances \nover eastern NM Tuesday night into Wednesday morning as it will be\nheavily dependent on the precise track and timing of the H5 low \nto NM's N and NW. \n\nAside from rain chances, rapidly cooling temperatures aloft \nassociated with the core of H5 low will increase snow chances over \nthe northern mountains Tuesday night into Wednesday morning. A light \ndusting could end up gracing elevations <10k feet. \n\nThereafter, cooler and drier conditions sweep west to east through \nthe state behind the H5 low's associated cold front. This will bring \ndaytime high temperatures to near or below normal across the state \nto end next week. Overnight low temperatures will fall as well with \nmany northern valley locations flirting with their first freezes of \nthe season.",
